---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import PersonalizedAdsPrivacy from '../../components/PersonalizedAdsPrivacy.astro';
import { rel, SUPPORT_EMAIL, LEGAL_NAME } from '../../consts';
const description = `Privacy policy for EconByte: Daily Economics by ${LEGAL_NAME}.`;
---
<Base title="Privacy Policy · EconByte: Daily Economics" description={description} path="/privacy/econbyte">
  <main class="page">
    <div class="wrap" style="max-width:720px;">
      <a class="back" href={rel('/privacy/')}>← All privacy policies</a>
      <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
      <p class="updated">EconByte: Daily Economics · Effective September 15, 2026</p>

      <h2>The short version</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>EconByte is a free economics-learning app. It's paid for by ads, optional one-time purchases and an optional EconByte Pro subscription. There are no accounts, and your progress and bookmarks stay on your device.</li>
        <li>When you first open the app, Apple asks two questions: may the app track you, and may it send notifications. Tapping “Allow” on tracking allows <strong>personalized ads</strong> and turns on anonymous <strong>usage analytics</strong> and <strong>crash reports</strong>. Otherwise ads are non-personalized, and analytics and crash reports stay off unless you turn them on.</li>
        <li><strong>No ads at all in the EEA, the UK or Switzerland.</strong></li>
        <li>We don't sell your data for money. Some US state laws count personalized ads as “selling” or “sharing”, and you can <a href="#us-state-privacy-rights">opt out at any time</a> by turning tracking off.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What changed on September 15, 2026</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Ads can now be personalized, but only for people who tap “Allow” on Apple's tracking prompt. Everyone else gets non-personalized ads.</li>
        <li>Besides Google AdMob, ads can now come from partner networks: AppLovin, Meta Audience Network, Unity Ads, Liftoff Monetize, Mintegral, InMobi and Pangle.</li>
        <li>This policy now states that there are no ads in the EEA, the UK or Switzerland, and that ads are non-personalized when the app can't read your region.</li>
        <li>New section on US state privacy rights (California, Virginia, Colorado, Connecticut, Utah, Texas, Oregon and others), including how to opt out.</li>
        <li>Updated to cover the tracking prompt at first launch, EconByte Pro, topic packs and the Daily Brief. We also removed hard-coded prices, which can change.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Versions of the app released before this date request non-personalized ads only.</p>

      <h2>Your choices at first launch</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Tracking (Apple's prompt).</strong> “Allow” allows personalized ads and turns on usage analytics and crash reports. “Ask App Not to Track” keeps ads non-personalized and leaves analytics and crash reports off. If you updated from an earlier version, your earlier analytics choices are kept.</li>
        <li><strong>Notifications (Apple's prompt).</strong> “Allow” turns on the optional daily reminder. “Don't Allow” leaves it off.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        You can change all of this later. EconByte's <strong>Settings</strong> has switches for <strong>Usage
        analytics</strong>, <strong>Crash reports</strong> and the <strong>Daily reminder</strong>. To change tracking, see
        <a href="#advertising">Advertising</a> below.
      </p>

      <h2>What stays on your device</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Your streak, daily-set and course progress, and bookmarks.</li>
        <li>Your purchase and subscription state.</li>
        <li>Your reminder, analytics and crash-report preferences.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>We don't receive any of this. Deleting the app removes it from your device.</p>

      <h2>Daily Brief</h2>
      <p>
        To show the Daily Brief, EconByte downloads the latest brief from our server, which is hosted by
        <a href="https://railway.com/legal/privacy" rel="noopener">Railway</a>. The request carries no account, no
        advertising or analytics ID, and nothing about you. Like any web request, it reveals your IP address to the
        server so the response can be delivered, and we don't use that address to identify you. The brief's content
        comes from official public sources and is the same for everyone.
      </p>

      <h2>Daily reminder</h2>
      <p>
        EconByte can send a local notification reminding you to do today's set. iOS asks your permission first, and
        you can change the time or turn it off in Settings. The reminder is scheduled on your device; no server is involved.
      </p>

      <h2>Usage analytics (PostHog)</h2>
      <p>
        When usage analytics is on, the app sends simple usage events to <strong>PostHog</strong>, our analytics
        provider. Examples are which screen you opened or that you finished a set. Each event comes with a random ID
        created for this install (you can reset it in Settings), the app version, device model and iOS version. We
        don't create a profile of you, and we never send your name, email or anything you type. Like any internet service,
        PostHog receives your IP address when the app connects, and it may use that to estimate a rough location.
        Analytics is never used for advertising. When you turn the switch off, collection stops. See
        <a href="https://posthog.com/privacy" rel="noopener">PostHog's privacy policy</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Crash reports (Sentry)</h2>
      <p>
        When crash reports are on, a crash or serious error sends a technical report to <strong>Sentry</strong>. It
        contains what went wrong in the code, the app version, device model and iOS version, so we can fix it. Crash
        reports don't include your name or contact details, and Sentry's option for collecting personal information is
        turned off. See <a href="https://sentry.io/privacy/" rel="noopener">Sentry's privacy policy</a>.
      </p>

      <PersonalizedAdsPrivacy
        appName="EconByte"
        placements="The free version shows a banner ad in the feed. It may also show a full-screen ad at a natural break, such as after you finish a set, never while you're reading a card."
        adFreeOptions="Buying Remove Ads, or having an active EconByte Pro subscription,"
      />

      <h2>Purchases and EconByte Pro</h2>
      <p>
        EconByte offers optional one-time purchases (Unlock All Topics, Remove Ads, topic packs and a pack bundle)
        and <strong>EconByte Pro</strong>, an auto-renewing monthly or annual subscription. Apple processes all of them.
        We never see or store your payment details, and Apple handles billing under
        <a href="https://www.apple.com/legal/privacy/">Apple's privacy policy</a>. You can manage or cancel Pro in your
        Apple Account's subscription settings, or from <strong>Settings → Manage Subscription</strong> in the app.
        Purchases can be restored on any device signed in to the same Apple Account from <strong>Settings → Restore Purchases</strong>.
      </p>

      <h2>How long data is kept</h2>
      <p>
        Data on your device stays until you delete the app. Analytics and crash data are kept only as long as they
        help us improve the app, then deleted under our PostHog and Sentry retention settings. We don't combine them
        with data from other sources or from our other apps.
      </p>

      <h2>Children</h2>
      <p>
        EconByte is a general-audience app. It isn't directed at children, and we don't knowingly collect personal
        information from children under 13. If you think a child has sent us personal information, email us and we'll delete it.
      </p>

      <h2>Changes</h2>
      <p>
        If this policy changes materially, the new version will be posted at this URL with a new effective date and
        a summary of what changed.
      </p>

      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>{LEGAL_NAME}, Colorado, USA. Questions or privacy requests: <a href={`mailto:${SUPPORT_EMAIL}`}>{SUPPORT_EMAIL}</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </main>
</Base>
